CI was red on 8 lanes via the release-readiness gate, and it WAS my
change (not just artifact staleness): lib/commands/_issue.js is a D20
bd-retirement hot-path surface, and my inline nudge added `.beads` and
"Beads issue" tokens there. That tripped TWO blockers — the
bd-hot-path-issue-commands scanner (release-readiness.js:463 counts
`.beads`/`bd` per line) and the kernel-backed check (line 1340 flags
`Beads issue`/`.beads` as a Beads-backed surface).

- lib/beads-nudge.js (new): the entire nudge — message text (which
  necessarily names the legacy `.beads` store) + empty-read detection +
  once-per-root guard — now lives in this neutral module. _issue.js
  calls a token-free helper, so the hot path scans clean (0 tokens).
- lib/commands/_issue.js: nudge block removed; imports
  maybeWarnUnmigratedBeads and calls it after the read. Comment
  reworded to drop the `.beads` token.
- docs/work/.../bd-call-site-kill-list.md: regenerated from the
  canonical generator (renderBdCallSiteAuditMarkdown(auditBdCallSites))
  so the D20 audit artifact records the new file layout; idempotent.

Tests: test/beads-nudge.test.js unit-covers the relocated helper;
the existing integration test (issue-path-beads-nudge) still passes
through _issue.js. release.test.js gate 7/7 green; affected suites
25/25; ESLint clean.

Refs: a5399f3d-28df-474e-b5f3-08af6bd7b388

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
